00:37:16.510 --> 00:37:21.940
When I say that notebook should
have ability to process input,

00:37:21.940 --> 00:37:24.340
I mean that you should
be able to override

00:37:24.340 --> 00:37:27.040
these variables from
outside of the notebooks

00:37:27.040 --> 00:37:29.350
without changing notebook.

00:37:29.350 --> 00:37:32.290
This is powerful concept that
again Papermill and Netflix

00:37:32.290 --> 00:37:34.660
has implemented already.

00:37:34.660 --> 00:37:36.820
That's great that
they have used--

00:37:36.820 --> 00:37:38.030
doesn't have the support.

00:37:38.030 --> 00:37:39.840
But with these
particular ability

00:37:39.840 --> 00:37:42.250
to override these
variables, you can actually

00:37:42.250 --> 00:37:44.800
implement full continues
integration system.

00:37:44.800 --> 00:37:50.320
Imagine a notebook, but when
you're sending this notebook

00:37:50.320 --> 00:37:53.050
to the Git, continues
integration system pulls

00:37:53.050 --> 00:37:56.210
this notebook,
overrides variables.

00:37:56.210 --> 00:37:58.850
For example, it overwrites
name of the table,

00:37:58.850 --> 00:38:00.610
and instead of the
production table

00:38:00.610 --> 00:38:04.210
it's using table with
mock small amount of data,

00:38:04.210 --> 00:38:07.480
and executing everything with
that small amount of data

00:38:07.480 --> 00:38:10.570
just to verify that the
notebook is working-- completely

00:38:10.570 --> 00:38:11.260
working.

00:38:11.260 --> 00:38:13.150
And if something
is broken, it pages

00:38:13.150 --> 00:38:16.120
whoever is on call, or whoever
is in charge of the build.

00:38:16.120 --> 00:38:21.040
All this is possible if you
do integrate this capability

00:38:21.040 --> 00:38:23.510
of input arguments.

00:38:23.510 --> 00:38:25.870
So this is effectively
covers the last two.

00:38:25.870 --> 00:38:28.155
Why it's important
to have a ability

00:38:28.155 --> 00:38:30.730
